# **Paper Critic — Fresh GitHub Code Audit**

Repository: [Kasi72/Paper-Critc](https://github.com/Kasi72/Paper-Critc?utm_source=chatgpt.com) · Latest inspected commit: [fd34c94](https://github.com/Kasi72/Paper-Critc/commit/fd34c94e5c073ec2541c3684b68b3af09169af3b?utm_source=chatgpt.com) · 26 November 2026 (IST)

I checked the latest `main` branch, compared it with the previously audited commit (`cae0a7d`), reviewed the changed Kotlin implementation and Firebase rules, and inspected the actual GitHub Actions failures.

**The new code fixes several major problems from the last audit. However, it still has build, evidence-integrity, and clinical-appraisal weaknesses that warrant correction before release.**

## **1. Confirmed release blocker: GitHub CI is failing**

P0 — Build pipeline broken

**Latest CI run: Failed**

[View failed GitHub Actions run](https://github.com/Kasi72/Paper-Critc/actions/runs/45674811260?utm_source=chatgpt.com)

The failure is specific and reproducible from the CI logs:

```
Execution failed for task ':app:validateSigningDebug'

Keystore file '.../Paper-Critc/debug.keystore'
not found for signing config 'debugConfig'.
```

The preceding CI run failed for the same reason.

**Root cause:** [app/build.gradle.kts](https://github.com/Kasi72/Paper-Critc/blob/fd34c94e5c073ec2541c3684b68b3af09169af3b/app/build.gradle.kts?utm_source=chatgpt.com#L28-L57) explicitly configures debug signing to use a local `debug.keystore` file, but that file isn't present in the GitHub Actions checkout.

**Recommended fix:** Remove the custom debug signing override from the Gradle `debug` build type, allowing Android Gradle Plugin to use its standard generated debug keystore. Preserve the separately configured release signing.

After that, run the build, unit tests and lint as separate CI steps, rather than one combined command. This makes it possible to tell a compilation failure from a test failure or lint issue.

**Important:** The CI failure occurs before the complete build-and-test workflow finishes. Therefore, passing tests reported from another environment do not establish that the current GitHub revision passes CI.

## **2. What has actually been fixed since the last audit**

The current source includes concrete repairs for several previously reported issues:

- **Account separation:** Room records now use owner scopes, including composite primary keys for account-owned reviews and API providers.
- **Anonymous review handling:** Local anonymous drafts require explicit adoption before account synchronization.
- **Provider privacy:** An unavailable custom API provider now produces an error rather than silently selecting Firebase.
- **Large evidence ledgers:** Findings are chunked and checksummed instead of truncated to 60,000 characters.
- **Legacy validation:** Unverified legacy reviews now default to `LEGACY_UNVALIDATED`.
- **Concurrent note and bookmark editing:** Dedicated field-level update paths replace some full-document overwrites.
- **PDF diagnostics:** The exporter now attempts to reopen and inspect the actual PDF bytes.
- **Clinical appraisal:** GRADE assessment includes additional evidence gates instead of immediately assigning certainty from general rubric scores.

These changes are meaningful, and the earlier audit's resolved findings should not be counted again.

## **3. Remaining critical defects in the latest code**

### **P0-02 — A manuscript can remain visible after switching accounts**

**Source:** [Workbench form state](https://github.com/Kasi72/Paper-Critc/blob/fd34c94e5c073ec2541c3684b68b3af09169af3b/app/src/main/java/com/example/ui/viewmodel/PaperCriticViewModel.kt?utm_source=chatgpt.com#L350-L356) and [Account-switch cleanup](https://github.com/Kasi72/Paper-Critc/blob/fd34c94e5c073ec2541c3684b68b3af09169af3b/app/src/main/java/com/example/ui/viewmodel/PaperCriticViewModel.kt?utm_source=chatgpt.com#L503-L549)

The new account-isolation logic clears cached reviews, the selected dossier, running analysis jobs and chat state. **It does not clear the manuscript input form.**

That form contains manuscript text, replication code, article metadata and rendered PDF page images.

If researcher A imports an unpublished manuscript and signs out, researcher B can inherit the manuscript still displayed in the workbench on the same installation.

There is a second race: document-import operations at [Manuscript and replication-code import](https://github.com/Kasi72/Paper-Critc/blob/fd34c94e5c073ec2541c3684b68b3af09169af3b/app/src/main/java/com/example/ui/viewmodel/PaperCriticViewModel.kt?utm_source=chatgpt.com#L789-L873) are not session-bound. A file import begun by A can finish after B signs in and populate B's form.

**Fix:** Make drafts account-scoped, clear the active form during account transitions, cancel outstanding import jobs, and check the initiating session before committing extracted content.

**Regression test:** Import a PDF as A, switch to B during extraction, and verify that neither extracted text nor images ever appear under B.

### **P0-03 — Clinical GRADE certainty can still be falsely reassuring**

**Source:** [Clinical certainty evaluator](https://github.com/Kasi72/Paper-Critc/blob/fd34c94e5c073ec2541c3684b68b3af09169af3b/app/src/main/java/com/example/data/ai/Reviewer2PipelineService.kt?utm_source=chatgpt.com#L3962-L4127)

The latest GRADE evaluator has better safeguards, but its decisions still rely heavily on the presence of particular phrases.

For example, a mention of `placebo` can count as adequate risk-of-bias controls. A reported sample size or confidence interval can make imprecision `NOT_SERIOUS`. The occurrence of `publication bias` or `PROSPERO` can contribute to marking publication bias `NOT_SERIOUS`, even if the text states that publication bias **was not assessed**.

For an RCT, a collection of such keyword matches can yield `HIGH_CERTAINTY` without independently establishing adequate risk-of-bias assessment, clinically meaningful precision or an outcome-specific evidence profile.

**Fix:** Do not equate mentioning an assessment with successfully passing it. Require structured, outcome-specific GRADE domain evidence, preferably including a PICO definition, study-level risk-of-bias judgments, effect estimates and explicit rationale for every downgrade decision. If those inputs are unavailable, withhold the certainty rating.

### **P0-04 — The report can label an opposing AI statement as verified**

**Source:** [Evidence-governed report assembly](https://github.com/Kasi72/Paper-Critc/blob/fd34c94e5c073ec2541c3684b68b3af09169af3b/app/src/main/java/com/example/data/ai/Reviewer2PipelineService.kt?utm_source=chatgpt.com#L3544-L3653)

The new report governance accepts statements linked to a verified finding ID and also automatically links sentences when enough words overlap with a verified finding.

Neither mechanism proves that the sentence expresses the **same proposition**.

For example, a finding asserting that a sensitivity analysis was absent and a sentence asserting that it was performed can share enough words to be linked, despite opposite meanings.

The separate quote-entailment function now checks some negation and direction patterns, but the report-assembly routine does not enforce equivalent semantic checks.

**Fix:** Treat finding IDs as pointers, not proof. Author verified report statements from validated, structured claims, or require a semantic consistency check for polarity, method status, outcome and scope before promoting AI prose to a verified statement. Unsupported prose must remain explicitly provisional.

### **P0-05 — Incomplete manuscript submissions receive an invented numerical score**

**Source:** [Document readiness failure result](https://github.com/Kasi72/Paper-Critc/blob/fd34c94e5c073ec2541c3684b68b3af09169af3b/app/src/main/java/com/example/data/ai/Reviewer2PipelineService.kt?utm_source=chatgpt.com#L817-L881)

When the document-readiness gate blocks a review before any LLM appraisal, it creates a `PaperReview` with a **50/100 credibility score** and all six rubric axes at **5/10**.

Those values have no evidentiary basis because the appraisal never took place. Although the verdict says insufficient evidence, the UI or a downstream export can still display an apparently meaningful score.

**Fix:** Represent unassessed scores as absent, not 50. Use a distinct blocked-analysis result type, or a nullable rubric with *Not Assessed* shown consistently in the app, database and PDF.

## **4. High-priority data integrity, privacy and reliability bugs**

### **P1-01 — A temporary Firestore error can make a verified report lose its evidence ledger**

**Source:** [Evidence chunk hydration](https://github.com/Kasi72/Paper-Critc/blob/fd34c94e5c073ec2541c3684b68b3af09169af3b/app/src/main/java/com/example/data/repository/ReviewRepository.kt?utm_source=chatgpt.com#L48-L74) and [Cloud review observation](https://github.com/Kasi72/Paper-Critc/blob/fd34c94e5c073ec2541c3684b68b3af09169af3b/app/src/main/java/com/example/data/repository/ReviewRepository.kt?utm_source=chatgpt.com#L144-L171)

The evidence-ledger chunking implementation correctly checks for missing or corrupted data.

But when hydration fails inside the archive listener, the exception is caught and the original, unhydrated `PaperReview` is returned.

That record can still have `validationStatus = VERIFIED` while its `structuredFindingsJson` is empty. The ViewModel can then cache this degraded record offline.

On a device without a previous intact local copy, this makes the report's evidence unavailable without clearly marking the review incomplete.

**Solution:** Introduce a `LedgerLoadState` (`COMPLETE`, `TEMPORARILY_UNAVAILABLE`, `CORRUPT`, `INCOMPLETE`). Never present an unhydrated report as fully verified. Retain the last known-good ledger and retry hydration separately.

### **P1-02 — Deleting a report can leave confidential evidence chunks in Firestore**

**Source:** [Review deletion](https://github.com/Kasi72/Paper-Critc/blob/fd34c94e5c073ec2541c3684b68b3af09169af3b/app/src/main/java/com/example/data/repository/ReviewRepository.kt?utm_source=chatgpt.com#L348-L373)

Before deleting a review, the repository tries to list its `findings_chunks` subcollection.

If that listing fails, the code substitutes an empty list and proceeds to delete the parent document.

Deleting a Firestore document does not automatically delete its subcollections. Accordingly, a failed chunk-list operation can leave the underlying manuscript evidence in cloud storage after the UI reports that the review was deleted.

A related problem exists in the cleanup of old chunks during document updates.

**Solution:** Do not swallow errors during destructive cleanup. Make the delete operation fail visibly until all child records can be removed, or use a trusted backend recursive-deletion operation. Test interrupted and permission-denied chunk enumeration.

### **P1-03 — Cloud refresh can overwrite newer local notes, bookmarks or report details**

**Source:** [Offline caching](https://github.com/Kasi72/Paper-Critc/blob/fd34c94e5c073ec2541c3684b68b3af09169af3b/app/src/main/java/com/example/data/repository/LocalReviewRepository.kt?utm_source=chatgpt.com#L45-L79) and [Automatic cloud refresh](https://github.com/Kasi72/Paper-Critc/blob/fd34c94e5c073ec2541c3684b68b3af09169af3b/app/src/main/java/com/example/ui/viewmodel/PaperCriticViewModel.kt?utm_source=chatgpt.com#L286-L297)

Although notes and bookmarks now have field-level updates, ordinary cloud refreshes still replace complete local Room rows.

Consider an offline note edit followed by a stale cloud snapshot. The cache routine protects certain ledger fields but has no general dirty-field or revision-based conflict management for notes and bookmarks.

The separate search-grounding routine can also cache an older `PaperReview` object after a long asynchronous operation.

**Solution:** Store user annotations independently, track pending writes and record revisions, and merge incoming cloud snapshots without overwriting locally modified fields. Use a durable outbox for failed cloud updates.

### **P1-04 — Statistical-claim truncation is still silent**

**Source:** [Claim ledger metadata](https://github.com/Kasi72/Paper-Critc/blob/fd34c94e5c073ec2541c3684b68b3af09169af3b/app/src/main/java/com/example/data/ai/stats/StatisticalClaimParser.kt?utm_source=chatgpt.com#L23-L37) and [Claim discovery and return](https://github.com/Kasi72/Paper-Critc/blob/fd34c94e5c073ec2541c3684b68b3af09169af3b/app/src/main/java/com/example/data/ai/stats/StatisticalClaimParser.kt?utm_source=chatgpt.com#L643-L695)

The latest implementation raised many extraction limits to 500 matches per statistical family. This is an improvement.

However, calls such as `.take(MAX_PER_FAMILY_DISCOVERY)` still discard later matches. The returned ledger defaults `truncatedClaimCount` to zero rather than computing the actual number omitted.

A lengthy manuscript could therefore be reported as statistically audited even though part of its claim inventory was never examined.

**Solution:** Count all discovered matches before applying limits. Preserve omitted-claim counts and page references. Prioritize primary outcomes, Results and critical tables. Any undisclosed truncation should prevent a claim of complete quantitative coverage.

### **P1-05 — Narrative report sections are still silently shortened during cloud saving**

**Source:** [Firestore report payload](https://github.com/Kasi72/Paper-Critc/blob/fd34c94e5c073ec2541c3684b68b3af09169af3b/app/src/main/java/com/example/data/repository/ReviewRepository.kt?utm_source=chatgpt.com#L463-L523)

The structured evidence ledger is now stored losslessly. But `formattedDossier`, `mathAuditReport`, `redTeamCritique`, `codeAuditReport` and other narrative fields still undergo fixed-length `.take(...)` operations.

This can cut away entire paragraphs of technical reasoning without notifying the user.

**Solution:** Apply the lossless chunking approach to long narrative sections as well, or store those sections as separate documents. A successfully synchronized report must preserve its full substantive text.

### **P1-06 — Account-switch safety is incomplete for secondary cloud operations**

**Source:** [Bookmark, notes and delete operations](https://github.com/Kasi72/Paper-Critc/blob/fd34c94e5c073ec2541c3684b68b3af09169af3b/app/src/main/java/com/example/ui/viewmodel/PaperCriticViewModel.kt?utm_source=chatgpt.com#L1082-L1174)

The main analysis pipeline now captures the initiating account and session generation. This is a valuable fix.

But secondary operations for bookmarking, notes and deletion launch coroutines that call repository methods resolving the currently signed-in user when the coroutine executes.

An account transition between launching the coroutine and making the Firestore request can make the request target a different account's collection than intended.

**Solution:** Require an expected account ID at the repository boundary and verify it before every remote mutation. Apply the same session-generation protection to all account-scoped work, including deletion and file imports.

### **P1-07 — Legacy API keys can become inaccessible after the account-isolation migration**

**Source:** [Room v5-to-v6 migration](https://github.com/Kasi72/Paper-Critc/blob/fd34c94e5c073ec2541c3684b68b3af09169af3b/app/src/main/java/com/example/data/local/PaperCriticSqlDatabase.kt?utm_source=chatgpt.com#L419-L469)

The migration puts existing API provider records into an anonymous ownership scope. Afterward, the signed-in user's provider list only queries that user's account scope.

This safely avoids silently assigning old credentials to the wrong person, but it can also make previously configured providers disappear after an upgrade.

**Solution:** Provide a one-time credential recovery or reassociation flow. Require explicit user confirmation before moving any legacy credential into an authenticated account.

